\begin_layout Standard
To build a bare-metal Ada driver for the CYW43439 from scratch,
 the first step is parsing the gSPI (generic SPI) hardware header and the SDPCM protocol header.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the Raspberry Pi Pico W the CYW43439 operates over a custom half-duplex SPI protocol (gSPI).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Every transaction requires a 32-bit Command Header sent by the host,
 followed by response or payload headers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The 32-Bit gSPI Command Header
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before reading any data the Ada driver must send a 32-bit command word to the chip.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This tells the CYW43439 whether you are reading or writing,
 which function block you are targeting and the register address.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
type SPI_Command is record
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Write_Mode :
 Boolean;
 -- True = Write,
 False = Read
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Auto_Inc :
 Boolean;
 -- True = Increment address automatically
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Function_Num :
 Unsigned_2;
 -- 0:
 Bus/gSPI,
 1:
 Backplane,
 2:
 WLAN Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Address :
 Unsigned_15;
 -- Register address or buffer offset
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Data_Length :
 Unsigned_13;
 -- Number of bytes to transfer
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
end record;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
pragma Pack (SPI_Command);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The 32-Bit gSPI Response Header
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When performing a Read operation the CYW43439 immediately replies with a 32-bit Status/Response header before delivering the requested payload.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Ada driver must read and validate this word to ensure the chip is ready.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
type SPI_Response is record
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Data_Not_Ready :
 Boolean;
 -- True if the chip needs more time (retry required)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Cmd_Error :
 Boolean;
 -- True if the previous command was invalid
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
WLAN_Interrupt :
 Boolean;
 -- True if WLAN data is pending
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Reserved :
 Unsigned_5;
 -- Hardware reserved bits
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Bus_Status :
 Unsigned_24;
 -- Internal status flags (e.g.,
 credit availability)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
end record;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
pragma Pack (SPI_Response);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The 12-Byte SDPCM Header
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once the hardware-level gSPI transfer is cleared and you are reading actual network data (Function 2),
 the payload begins with the SDPCM (Serial Data Port Control Message) header.
 This header tells your network stack what kind of packet has arrived.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
type SDPCM_Header is record
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Framelen_Low :
 Interfaces.Unsigned_8;
 -- Total frame length (Low Byte)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Framelen_High :
 Interfaces.Unsigned_8;
 -- Total frame length (High Byte)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Check_Low :
 Interfaces.Unsigned_8;
 -- Bitwise NOT of Framelen_Low
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Check_High :
 Interfaces.Unsigned_8;
 -- Bitwise NOT of Framelen_High
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Channel :
 Interfaces.Unsigned_8;
 -- 0:
 Control,
 1:
 Event,
 2:
 Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Sequence :
 Interfaces.Unsigned_8;
 -- Packet sequence number
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Pad_Bytes :
 Interfaces.Unsigned_8;
 -- Number of padding bytes added
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Reserved :
 Interfaces.Unsigned_8;
 -- Reserved for internal alignment
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Don_Care :
 Interfaces.Unsigned_32;
 -- 4 bytes of hardware-specific padding
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
end record;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
pragma Pack (SDPCM_Header);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Implementation Steps in Ada
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To implement the basic read sequence in your bare-metal loop:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Assert CS Low:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Lower the Chip Select line.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Send Command:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Transmit the 32-bit SPI_Command (configured for Read,
 Function 2).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Read Status:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Immediately read back the 32-bit SPI_Response.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Check Readiness:
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Loop or delay if Data_Not_Ready is True.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Stream Payload:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Read the 12-byte SDPCM_Header,
 parse the Framelen and stream the remaining bytes into your network buffer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Assert CS High:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Raise the Chip Select line to conclude the transaction.
\end_layout

